faq:
  - q: "When should you follow up after a job interview?"
    a: "Two stages: (1) Thank you email within 24 hours of the interview — personalized, referencing something specific from the conversation. (2) Status follow-up if you have not heard back by the timeline they gave you — or after 5 business days if no timeline was provided. Most candidates skip the first and become anxious about the second. Both are expected and professional."
  - q: "How do you follow up after an interview without being annoying?"
    a: "Follow up once, professionally, with a specific reference. 'Following up on the [Role] interview from [date]. I remain very interested — particularly in [specific thing discussed]. Happy to provide any additional information that would be helpful.' This is appropriate and expected. Following up every 2 days is annoying. One substantive follow-up after the expected timeline is not."
  - q: "What do you say in a follow-up email after an interview?"
    a: "Three components: (1) Reference the specific role and date of the interview. (2) Restate your interest with one specific reason — not generic enthusiasm. (3) Make a clear ask: asking about next steps or the timeline. Example: 'I'm following up on my interview for [Role] on [date]. I remain genuinely interested — especially after learning more about [specific thing]. Could you share an update on your timeline or next steps?'"
  - q: "How many times should you follow up after an interview?"
    a: "Maximum two follow-ups: the thank you email within 24 hours, and one status follow-up if you have not heard back within the stated timeline (or 5–7 business days). If you have still not heard back after two attempts, send one final message: 'I understand you may be in the middle of the process. If the role is still active, I remain interested. If not, I appreciate the time.' Then move on."
  - q: "Should you follow up by email or phone after an interview?"
    a: "Email — unless you have an existing phone relationship with the recruiter or the company is known to communicate primarily by phone. Email creates a written record, gives the recipient time to respond, and does not create the pressure of an unexpected call. If you have a recruiter's direct number and a warm relationship, a brief call is fine — but lead with email."
